Jove wrote:

>   You've listed a lot of reasons why Google is convenient, but
>   no reasons to show that it's good.

Being convenient isn't a reason in itself?

I can only use Google most of the time now as well.
Historically, I used GNUS for the longest time, and now
generally use Thunderbird when I can get a direct news feed.
But most of the time, I don't have a news feed, so it's Google.

>   Convenient is not the same as good.

It's a good attribute.  By definition (my definition, at least),
a newsreader that I can use is better than one I cannot.

>   Why is Google the enemy?

>   Threading by subject.  Duplicate subjects get lost in the mists of
> time.  (And you can no longer really sort posts by date to find the
> latest ones.  You used to be able to do that.)  As more and more
> subjects get added, more and more new posts will duplicate previous
> subjects, and will essentially be lost forever.  (I've started being
> careful about making the subjects of my new posts unique.)

I don't like threading by subject either, but I can't see where
it has made me loose any posts.  I'm not sure if I had to set up
anything special, when I first connected to Google, but I
currently see threads sorted by the most recent post in the
thread.  Google may think that the post belongs to a thread over
a year old, but that thread is presented, as containing unread
posts, at the top of my list.

>   Difficult to autoquote in a reply.

I don't find it very difficult.  You click on show options, then
reply, and the message is autoquoted.  That's two clicks -- it's
not as good as the alternatives: Thunderbird is a single click,
and with GNUS, I didn't even have to take my hands off the
keyboard.  But it's not the end of the world, either.

>   "Tree" view convinces too many posters that "all newsreaders must be
> like this."

Aren't they?  GNUS and Thunderbird are.  I would consider it a
major defect if a newsreader didn't support this.

> Their posts of consisting of single word replies to unquoted
> posts make them look like prats, even on Google if you don't
> use tree view.  (And Google will archive the evidence
> forever.)

>   No automatic signature support(?).  So signature delimiters
>   must be typed in every time.  These are subject to typos at
>   the very least, missing " " after the "--", etc.

A pain, agreed.  On the other hand: both Firefox (my browser)
and Thunderbird have totally unusable editors, so I always end
up copy-pasting the response into gvim, editing my comments in
there, and then copy-pasting it back into the
browser/newsreader.  And it's pretty easy to add a macro to
append the signature in any decent editor.

>   Shows messages in proportional font.  Yes, a single click
>   can take you to fixed font, with more download wait.  And
>   more ads, which I suspect is the real point.

The "single click" is once; Google then remembers your
preference, and uses fixed font from then on.  At least, I see
everything in fixed font.

>   Ridiculously narrow width for display so more ads can be
>   shown on the page.  (I presume that's the reason.  I block
>   the ads.)

Looks like the usual display width to me.

>   The only way to see a message as it was written by the
>   author (and presumably the way the author intended it to be
>   seen) is to click "Show Original."  I suspect few posters
>   using Google do this.  So they may never learn how their own
